1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,919
Italy has a Kafkaesque bureaucracy, but the real culprit is not the lazy clerk.

2
00:00:05,919 --> 00:00:11,599
The real short circuit begins in Parliament, when governments last too little.

3
00:00:11,599 --> 00:00:17,559
Think of a Second Republic politician: with elections at the door, he must simulate activism,

4
00:00:17,559 --> 00:00:22,160
so he hastily passes dozens of complex, poorly written reforms.

5
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:27,560
This avalanche of rules overwhelms public offices, paralyzing the public administration.

6
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:32,880
But for the politician this paralysis becomes a perfect shield,

7
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:37,240
because it delays the moment when citizens will discover the flaws in the law.

8
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:39,200
The paradox is fierce.

9
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:45,640
Thanks to bureaucratic slowness, incompetent rulers are incentivized to churn out junk laws.

10
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:50,840
They cash in consensus today, before the system reveals their failure tomorrow.

11
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:54,600
Economists call it a Kafkaesque steady state.

12
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:59,960
Inefficiency drives the passage of new laws that multiply inefficiency.

13
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:03,560
A vicious circle in which politics saves itself by locking up the country.

14
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:08,280
That is why the perennial instability of the Second Republic has turned the Italian state

15
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:10,760
into today's inextricable labyrinth.

